\section{Addressing a row}{

\code{term} matches, in this order, the \code{term} column, the \code{label} column,
and a term with the brms class prefix removed (\code{"wt"} finds \code{b_wt}).
For a hypotheses table it matches \code{hypothesis}. A structural-equation
path is addressed by its two sides: \code{apa_inline(x, "visual", "x1", op = "=~")}; the order of the sides does not matter for a covariance
(\verb{~~}) and does for every other operator. \code{op} alone finds an
intercept (\code{op = "~1"}). \code{group} restricts the search first, for a
multi-group fit or a grouped hypothesis. \code{term = NULL} reports every
row. No match, or more than one, is an error that lists the
candidates.
}

\section{What is printed}{

A \code{parameters} row prints the estimate, its interval labelled by the
row's own \code{ci_method} (\code{CrI} for an equal-tailed credible interval,
\code{HDI}, \code{CI} for a Wald or bootstrap interval), then whichever of the
probability of direction, the ROPE share, the Bayes factor and the p
value the row carries. A population-level regression coefficient
